<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emden</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emden is a port city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-frisia">East Frisia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, near the mouth of the Ems on the Dollart bay. In the 16th century it received many Protestant refugees from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and became a centre of the Reformed Church, sometimes called the '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> of the North'. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> support, long resisted the counts and princes of East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frisia">Frisia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emden.jpg" alt="Emden" width="320" height="210" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bin im Garten · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flug_Emden_2010_191.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
